1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,262
What does a three-D-printed hypercar really cost?

2
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:06,298
The reported price of a Czinger 21C

3
00:00:06,298 --> 00:00:09,154
is one point seven million dollars.

4
00:00:09,334 --> 00:00:11,315
But that is the price tag,

5
00:00:11,315 --> 00:00:12,306
not the cost.

6
00:00:12,486 --> 00:00:15,155
The cost includes thousands of hours of

7
00:00:15,155 --> 00:00:18,029
labour, a company that has raised hundreds

8
00:00:18,029 --> 00:00:19,603
of millions of dollars,

9
00:00:19,603 --> 00:00:20,903
and years of delay.

10
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:23,624
We pulled every number our sources give,

11
00:00:23,624 --> 00:00:25,402
and we show where they stop.

12
00:00:25,753 --> 00:00:28,593
Our sources are Backfire News,

13
00:00:28,593 --> 00:00:33,423
via Yahoo, from March twenty twenty-four; GTPlanet,

14
00:00:33,423 --> 00:00:38,063
from February twenty twenty; Wikipedia's pages on

15
00:00:38,063 --> 00:00:41,662
the Czinger 21C and the McLaren Senna;

16
00:00:41,662 --> 00:00:44,503
CSQ; ASME; Autocar and GoAuto.

17
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:46,426
Where numbers conflict,

18
00:00:46,426 --> 00:00:47,411
we show both.

19
00:00:47,591 --> 00:00:49,826
Our own arithmetic is labelled,

20
00:00:49,826 --> 00:00:52,349
and anything the sources do not say

21
00:00:52,349 --> 00:00:54,151
is flagged as not stated.

22
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,894
Start with the sticker.

23
00:00:56,074 --> 00:00:57,981
The Backfire News report,

24
00:00:57,981 --> 00:01:01,263
published on the nineteenth of March twenty

25
00:01:01,263 --> 00:01:04,239
twenty-four, gives a price of one point

26
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:07,444
seven million dollars for the Czinger 21C,

27
00:01:07,444 --> 00:01:10,725
a tandem two-seater limited to eighty cars.

28
00:01:10,905 --> 00:01:13,325
That is the only explicit price in

29
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:15,674
the sources we read from the last

30
00:01:15,674 --> 00:01:16,385
two years.

31
00:01:16,565 --> 00:01:18,814
We could not find a Czinger price

32
00:01:18,814 --> 00:01:19,154
list.

33
00:01:19,504 --> 00:01:21,009
Four years earlier,

34
00:01:21,009 --> 00:01:22,987
the number was different.

35
00:01:23,167 --> 00:01:25,755
GTPlanet, in February twenty twenty,

36
00:01:25,755 --> 00:01:27,983
said the price was unconfirmed,

37
00:01:27,983 --> 00:01:30,283
and told readers not to expect a

38
00:01:30,283 --> 00:01:32,583
change from two million dollars.

39
00:01:32,763 --> 00:01:33,913
By our arithmetic,

40
00:01:33,913 --> 00:01:36,722
that is three hundred thousand dollars above

41
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:37,808
the later report,

42
00:01:37,808 --> 00:01:40,426
a difference of about eighteen percent of

43
00:01:40,426 --> 00:01:41,958
one point seven million.

44
00:01:42,308 --> 00:01:44,622
Why the number moved is not stated

45
00:01:44,622 --> 00:01:45,234
anywhere.

46
00:01:45,414 --> 00:01:47,540
It might be that the early figure

47
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:48,828
was a rounded guess,

48
00:01:48,828 --> 00:01:51,083
that the company changed the price,

49
00:01:51,083 --> 00:01:54,110
or that two writers used different assumptions.

50
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:56,270
We can only say the price has

51
00:01:56,270 --> 00:01:58,659
been reported at two million and at

52
00:01:58,659 --> 00:02:00,297
one point seven million,

53
00:02:00,297 --> 00:02:01,594
in different years,

54
00:02:01,594 --> 00:02:04,119
and that neither source cites a price

55
00:02:04,119 --> 00:02:04,460
list.

56
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:06,913
If every one of the eighty cars

57
00:02:06,913 --> 00:02:08,609
sold at the lower figure,

58
00:02:08,609 --> 00:02:10,848
then by our arithmetic the run is

59
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:14,307
worth about one hundred thirty-six million dollars.

60
00:02:14,487 --> 00:02:15,643
At two million,

61
00:02:15,643 --> 00:02:18,110
it is one hundred sixty million.

62
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,773
That is a simple multiplication,

63
00:02:20,773 --> 00:02:22,403
not a revenue figure:

64
00:02:22,403 --> 00:02:24,808
the sources do not say how many

65
00:02:24,808 --> 00:02:25,895
cars are sold,

66
00:02:25,895 --> 00:02:28,456
whether prices differ by version,

67
00:02:28,456 --> 00:02:30,085
or what options cost.

68
00:02:30,435 --> 00:02:33,868
The price per horsepower is another way

69
00:02:33,868 --> 00:02:34,748
to see it,

70
00:02:34,748 --> 00:02:35,892
with caveats.

71
00:02:36,072 --> 00:02:38,658
At one point seven million dollars and

72
00:02:38,658 --> 00:02:41,653
Wikipedia's twelve hundred fifty horsepower,

73
00:02:41,653 --> 00:02:44,987
our arithmetic gives about thirteen hundred sixty

74
00:02:44,987 --> 00:02:46,893
dollars for each horsepower.

75
00:02:47,073 --> 00:02:48,483
At two million,

76
00:02:48,483 --> 00:02:49,987
sixteen hundred.

77
00:02:50,167 --> 00:02:54,070
Using Backfire's own thirteen thirty-one horsepower,

78
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:56,622
it is about twelve hundred eighty.

79
00:02:56,802 --> 00:02:59,238
The answer depends on which source you

80
00:02:59,238 --> 00:02:59,623
trust.

81
00:02:59,973 --> 00:03:02,686
Here is the number that gets closest

82
00:03:02,686 --> 00:03:03,665
to real cost.

83
00:03:03,845 --> 00:03:07,351
Backfire News says each Czinger 21C takes

84
00:03:07,351 --> 00:03:10,770
three thousand to four thousand hours of

85
00:03:10,770 --> 00:03:11,369
labour.

86
00:03:11,549 --> 00:03:14,022
The article does not say what the

87
00:03:14,022 --> 00:03:16,721
hours include, such as machine time,

88
00:03:16,721 --> 00:03:19,195
assembly, engineering or testing,

89
00:03:19,195 --> 00:03:21,219
and it does not give a cost

90
00:03:21,219 --> 00:03:21,893
per hour.

91
00:03:22,073 --> 00:03:23,997
But it is the clearest measure of

92
00:03:23,997 --> 00:03:24,871
effort we have.

93
00:03:25,221 --> 00:03:26,870
Put it in human terms.

94
00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:28,463
A full-time worker,

95
00:03:28,463 --> 00:03:29,801
by our arithmetic,

96
00:03:29,801 --> 00:03:32,774
works about two thousand eighty hours in

97
00:03:32,774 --> 00:03:35,153
a year, using forty hours a week

98
00:03:35,153 --> 00:03:36,640
for fifty-two weeks.

99
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:39,045
So one car is roughly one and

100
00:03:39,045 --> 00:03:41,731
a half to two person-years of work.

101
00:03:41,911 --> 00:03:44,624
That sounds enormous for a car sold

102
00:03:44,624 --> 00:03:45,864
to one customer,

103
00:03:45,864 --> 00:03:48,499
but remember the cars are built by

104
00:03:48,499 --> 00:03:50,282
teams, not individuals.

105
00:03:50,631 --> 00:03:52,210
Now the whole run.

106
00:03:52,390 --> 00:03:54,934
Eighty cars at three thousand to four

107
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:57,958
thousand hours is two hundred forty thousand

108
00:03:57,958 --> 00:04:00,639
to three hundred twenty thousand hours,

109
00:04:00,639 --> 00:04:01,876
by our arithmetic.

110
00:04:02,056 --> 00:04:04,692
Divided by two thousand eighty hours a

111
00:04:04,692 --> 00:04:07,051
year, that is a hundred fifteen to

112
00:04:07,051 --> 00:04:10,590
a hundred fifty-four full-time person-years of work

113
00:04:10,590 --> 00:04:12,116
for the planned total,

114
00:04:12,116 --> 00:04:13,643
before the Blackbirds.

115
00:04:13,992 --> 00:04:15,746
Can a small company do that?

116
00:04:15,926 --> 00:04:19,774
CSQ reported in September twenty twenty-two that

117
00:04:19,774 --> 00:04:23,623
Czinger Vehicles had fewer than fifty employees.

118
00:04:23,803 --> 00:04:25,022
By our arithmetic,

119
00:04:25,022 --> 00:04:27,596
fifty people give about a hundred four

120
00:04:27,596 --> 00:04:29,086
thousand hours a year.

121
00:04:29,266 --> 00:04:31,529
So two hundred forty to three hundred

122
00:04:31,529 --> 00:04:34,281
twenty thousand hours would take them roughly

123
00:04:34,281 --> 00:04:36,177
two and a third to three years,

124
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:38,379
if they did all the work themselves.

125
00:04:38,729 --> 00:04:41,632
That calculation is only an illustration.

126
00:04:41,812 --> 00:04:44,181
The sources do not say who does

127
00:04:44,181 --> 00:04:46,779
the hours, how many people work on

128
00:04:46,779 --> 00:04:47,925
the cars today,

129
00:04:47,925 --> 00:04:51,746
or whether sister company Divergent supplies parts

130
00:04:51,746 --> 00:04:52,586
and labour.

131
00:04:52,766 --> 00:04:54,960
But it shows why timing matters.

132
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:56,305
At that effort,

133
00:04:56,305 --> 00:04:57,702
by our arithmetic,

134
00:04:57,702 --> 00:05:00,265
a company of that size would need

135
00:05:00,265 --> 00:05:02,594
several years for eighty cars,

136
00:05:02,594 --> 00:05:05,933
and the sources say deliveries were planned

137
00:05:05,933 --> 00:05:07,563
across several years.

138
00:05:07,912 --> 00:05:09,100
By our arithmetic,

139
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:11,012
here is another illustration.

140
00:05:11,192 --> 00:05:13,486
If the whole one point seven million

141
00:05:13,486 --> 00:05:15,652
dollars were spent on those hours,

142
00:05:15,652 --> 00:05:18,327
it would be about four hundred twenty-five

143
00:05:18,327 --> 00:05:21,130
to five hundred sixty-seven dollars an hour.

144
00:05:21,310 --> 00:05:24,737
In reality, the price also covers parts,

145
00:05:24,737 --> 00:05:29,020
printing, development, profit and everything else.

146
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,380
We offer the figure as a ceiling,

147
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:32,636
not an hourly rate.

148
00:05:32,985 --> 00:05:35,545
The pitch for printed construction is that

149
00:05:35,545 --> 00:05:36,643
it avoids tooling.

150
00:05:36,823 --> 00:05:40,096
ASME says printed parts let engineers make,

151
00:05:40,096 --> 00:05:43,065
test and improve a part without waiting

152
00:05:43,065 --> 00:05:46,034
for tooling, and that the process frees

153
00:05:46,034 --> 00:05:49,003
designs from limits such as draft angle

154
00:05:49,003 --> 00:05:50,221
and tool access.

155
00:05:50,401 --> 00:05:52,627
That is a saving in time and

156
00:05:52,627 --> 00:05:53,582
flexibility.

157
00:05:53,762 --> 00:05:55,896
The article does not give a dollar

158
00:05:55,896 --> 00:05:57,465
figure for what it saves.

159
00:05:57,816 --> 00:06:00,923
Tooling matters most when you build many

160
00:06:00,923 --> 00:06:02,088
identical cars.

161
00:06:02,268 --> 00:06:04,659
A stamped steel panel has a huge

162
00:06:04,659 --> 00:06:07,574
up-front cost, then each part is cheap.

163
00:06:07,754 --> 00:06:09,549
For an eighty-car run,

164
00:06:09,549 --> 00:06:12,569
the up-front cost is spread over very

165
00:06:12,569 --> 00:06:13,303
few cars.

166
00:06:13,483 --> 00:06:15,267
That logic is our reading,

167
00:06:15,267 --> 00:06:17,051
not a quote from a source,

168
00:06:17,051 --> 00:06:19,658
but it explains why a printed approach

169
00:06:19,658 --> 00:06:21,854
could make sense for a hypercar.

170
00:06:22,204 --> 00:06:24,364
But printing has its own cost.

171
00:06:24,544 --> 00:06:28,652
Facfox reports that Divergent bought five pre-production

172
00:06:28,652 --> 00:06:31,806
metal printers from SLM Solutions in twenty

173
00:06:31,806 --> 00:06:35,620
nineteen and planned twenty next-generation systems.

174
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:38,016
The sources give no price for these

175
00:06:38,016 --> 00:06:38,586
machines.

176
00:06:38,766 --> 00:06:42,209
ASME also says early parts suffered from

177
00:06:42,209 --> 00:06:45,651
porosity and print-orientation problems.

178
00:06:45,831 --> 00:06:48,949
Fixing that took development effort that is

179
00:06:48,949 --> 00:06:50,545
part of the real cost.

180
00:06:50,895 --> 00:06:52,624
Then there is the money raised.

181
00:06:52,804 --> 00:06:56,651
CSQ's September twenty twenty-two article quotes Kevin

182
00:06:56,651 --> 00:07:00,070
Czinger saying Divergent recently brought in two

183
00:07:00,070 --> 00:07:02,207
hundred fifty million dollars,

184
00:07:02,207 --> 00:07:05,626
and that Czinger Vehicles and Divergent together

185
00:07:05,626 --> 00:07:08,617
have raised close to five hundred million.

186
00:07:08,797 --> 00:07:12,714
It names investors including John Thornton and

187
00:07:12,714 --> 00:07:13,650
Tom Steyer.

188
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,382
A careful point.

189
00:07:15,562 --> 00:07:16,914
By our arithmetic,

190
00:07:16,914 --> 00:07:20,370
five hundred million dollars divided by eighty

191
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:22,925
cars is six point two five million

192
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:23,976
dollars a car,

193
00:07:23,976 --> 00:07:26,531
which is nearly four times the one

194
00:07:26,531 --> 00:07:28,484
point seven million price.

195
00:07:28,664 --> 00:07:31,049
But that comparison is unfair,

196
00:07:31,049 --> 00:07:35,103
because the money funds Divergent's wider business,

197
00:07:35,103 --> 00:07:36,454
not only the 21C.

198
00:07:36,634 --> 00:07:38,598
The sources do not say how the

199
00:07:38,598 --> 00:07:39,711
funds were split.

200
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:42,533
What the comparison does show is scale.

201
00:07:42,713 --> 00:07:45,075
A car priced at one point seven

202
00:07:45,075 --> 00:07:48,123
million dollars is only the visible part

203
00:07:48,123 --> 00:07:50,256
of a project funded at a far

204
00:07:50,256 --> 00:07:51,247
larger level.

205
00:07:51,427 --> 00:07:54,520
CSQ also says Divergent had about five

206
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:56,392
hundred twenty patents,

207
00:07:56,392 --> 00:07:58,590
according to Kevin Czinger.

208
00:07:58,770 --> 00:08:01,534
The article does not value the patents,

209
00:08:01,534 --> 00:08:03,519
and we do not guess a value.

210
00:08:03,868 --> 00:08:05,798
To judge a hypercar price,

211
00:08:05,798 --> 00:08:07,282
it helps to compare.

212
00:08:07,462 --> 00:08:10,996
McLaren's Senna was announced in December twenty

213
00:08:10,996 --> 00:08:14,603
seventeen at seven hundred fifty thousand pounds,

214
00:08:14,603 --> 00:08:16,959
according to Autocar and GoAuto.

215
00:08:17,139 --> 00:08:19,779
GoAuto converted that to about one point

216
00:08:19,779 --> 00:08:21,957
three million Australian dollars.

217
00:08:22,137 --> 00:08:25,298
Wikipedia says the Senna was limited to

218
00:08:25,298 --> 00:08:26,758
five hundred cars.

219
00:08:27,107 --> 00:08:30,107
Autocar adds that buyers can add extras

220
00:08:30,107 --> 00:08:32,799
through McLaren Special Operations,

221
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:35,722
